{"step_index":87,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:35:54Z","thinking":"**Identifying Relevant Records**\n\nThe goal is to determine the number of records mentioning specific keywords: proposals (\"Quiet Return\" or \"Harbor H2\"), identifiers (\"QR7\", \"H1\", \"H2\"), individuals (\"Sam, product lead\"), topical terms (\"summer-offers\"), and process names (\"Current handoff\"). Reviewing the relevant document again to confirm context.\n\n\n**Evaluating Experiment Potential**\n\nReviewing available data, Quiet Return showed promise prior to summer, while Harbor was negative, and both designs have been refreshed. Revenue responsibility mandates prioritizing refund-adjusted revenue over trial starts. Prioritize records from the summer cleanup period to assess current performance.\n\n\n**Reviewing Quiet Return History**\n\nThe \"Quiet Return\" initiative, identified as record-b7ab79e22e, originated in summer-offers as QR7, a 20% discount for US returning iOS annual subscribers.  Deployed on 2026-07-12 with 50% exposure and a trial start +11%, it was rolled back on 2026-07-21 due to unresolved refund escalations, as per record-e0da7df86e. The initial positive trial start in record-da3a9e962e contrasts with the subsequent rollback, making deeper understanding necessary.\n\n\n**Analyzing Quiet Return Performance**\n\nPost-summer cleanup analysis shows that the initial positive trial starts of the Quiet Return initiative, QR7, masked a 6.2% decrease in refund-adjusted net revenue. Design changes merely updated illustration colors, leaving the underlying detrimental economic elements of the offer unchanged. Reuse of the name for a different cohort requires careful evaluation due to the negative prior result.\n\n\n**Clarifying Revenue Reporting**\n\nThe revenue report's +4% figure requires careful interpretation, as it excludes the previous Quiet Return initiative and other specific exposures, clarifying that a conflicting positive Quiet Return result was for a distinct desktop monthly subscriber offer. Now reviewing Harbor experiment details, given its negative prior results.\n\n**Investigating Harbor Deployment**\n\nHarbor, or Anchor plan picker (H1), was deployed to new US mobile accounts on August 10 and rolled back on August 18. Initial reports noted a -9% revenue impact, potentially linked to sessions being incorrectly counted as new or the annual plan resetting after the OS permission dialogue.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":false,"MatchPerLine":true,"Query":"record-d3b97d8b03","SearchPath":"/workspace/product","toolAction":"Searching for record-d3b97d8b03","toolSummary":"Search record-d3b97d8b03"}}]}
